{"Service.Check consults permDenialCache before re-reading permissions and returns an immediate deny; if a user later gains permission (e.g., by creating the resource), they can still be denied until the cache TTL expires, contradicting the PR goal of allowing creators to access newly created dashboards/folders immediately": 0.8276746273040771, "The userPermDenialCacheKey function uses underscore as delimiter between action, name, and parent fields, but these fields can contain underscores, causing key collisions where different (name, parent) combinations produce identical cache keys and incorrect authorization denials": 0.5704783201217651, "The denial cache key is built using checkReq.UserUID (raw UID from subject string) while the permission cache key uses userIdentifiers.UID (resolved canonical UID from DB), making the denial cache optimization ineffective for numeric-ID subjects": 0.32585418224334717, "Service.Check treats any successful permDenialCache lookup as a deny regardless of the stored boolean value, which is semantically fragile and makes the cache behavior dependent on key existence rather than value": 0.3706195056438446, "When getCachedIdentityPermissions returns a cache hit but checkPermission returns allowed=false, the code falls through and increments permissionCacheUsage with label 'false' (cache miss), causing cache hit rate metric to appear lower than actual": 0.9433680772781372, "Any error from getCachedIdentityPermissions including real DB errors from GetUserIdentifiers is silently treated as a cache miss and falls through to getIdentityPermissions, which calls GetUserIdentifiers again, doubling DB load during failures and swallowing the error without logging": 0.8955539464950562}